  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:51:11.784Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSubstituted filing of income tax returns is allowed for individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from a single employer, provided that the income tax has been correctly withheld by the employer. The certificate of withholding from the employer, stamped by the BIR, serves as the substituted filing.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provision is found in **Section 51-A of the NIRC**, which states the conditions for substituted filing of income tax returns by employees receiving purely compensation income.\n\n### Interpretation\nAccording to **Section 51-A of the NIRC**, individual taxpayers who receive purely compensation income from only one employer in the Philippines for the calendar year, where the tax due equals the tax withheld, are not required to file an annual income tax return. Instead, the certificate of withholding issued by the employer, when duly stamped by the BIR, is considered sufficient for compliance.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis provision simplifies the tax compliance process for employees with straightforward income situations, reducing the administrative burden of filing a return when the employer has already withheld the correct amount of tax. It ensures that employees can rely on their employer's withholding certificate as proof of tax compliance, streamlining the process for both taxpayers and the BIR.",
